    "note": [
        {
            "structuredValue": [],
            "parallelValue": [],
            "groupedValue": [],
            "value": "Our lab has recently demonstrated that optogenetic neuronal stimulation of either the ipsilesional motor cortex (iM1) or contralesional cerebellar dentate nucleus (cDN) can enhance post-stroke recovery. Interestingly, in both models neuronal stimulation downregulates mRNA expression of neuronal nitric oxide synthase (nNOS), an enzyme responsible for the production of nitric oxide (NO). Previously, activation of nNOS has been shown to worsen ischemic damage in acute stroke however, its role in stroke recovery is unclear. Here, using a mouse stroke model, we investigated the effects of pharmacological inhibition of nNOS on functional recovery after stroke. The experimental group received a pharmacological nNOS inhibitor (ARL 17477 dihydrochloride; 10 mg/kg and 1 mg/kg) daily from post-stroke day 5-14; the control group received saline. Their motor-sensory recovery was evaluated using a horizontal rotating beam test. The mice were sacrificed and brains removed for histology and western blots. Stroke mice treated with 10 mg/kg of nNOS inhibitor were found to recover significantly worse than vehicle treated mice; stroke mice treated with 1 mg/kg showed similar recovery results to vehicle treated mice. These results demonstrate a dosage-dependent negative correlation between nNOS inhibition and stroke recovery.",
            "type": "abstract",
            "identifier": [],
            "note": [],
            "appliesTo": []
        },
